\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Estimated smooth functions $g_j(\cdot )$ from the fitted GAM (Equation~\ref {eq:gam}) for each orthogonalized macro predictor. Axes show the residualized, orthogonalized predictor (horizontal) and the partial contribution to the fitted price (vertical). Because the predictors are orthogonalized, $g_1$ reflects the variation attributable to GDP after removing nothing (GDP is first in the ordering); $g_2$ reflects CPI after projecting out GDP; and $g_3$ reflects PFCE after projecting out both. The approximately linear shapes of $g_2$ and $g_3$ indicate that the penalized B-spline defaulted to a near-linear fit for those predictors under the chosen penalty; this does not invalidate the GAM framework but indicates weak evidence for non-linearity in those components under the current specification.}}{5}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:smooth}{{2}{5}{Estimated smooth functions $g_j(\cdot )$ from the fitted GAM (Equation~\ref {eq:gam}) for each orthogonalized macro predictor. Axes show the residualized, orthogonalized predictor (horizontal) and the partial contribution to the fitted price (vertical). Because the predictors are orthogonalized, $g_1$ reflects the variation attributable to GDP after removing nothing (GDP is first in the ordering); $g_2$ reflects CPI after projecting out GDP; and $g_3$ reflects PFCE after projecting out both. The approximately linear shapes of $g_2$ and $g_3$ indicate that the penalized B-spline defaulted to a near-linear fit for those predictors under the chosen penalty; this does not invalidate the GAM framework but indicates weak evidence for non-linearity in those components under the current specification}{figure.2}{}}
